There's an on-again off-again discussion about conducts around here,
involving whether a player has to choose a conduct at the outset of
the game to play for it to "count."

I'm going to use "genocideless" as the hypothetical conduct. Let's
consider four situations:

1)  A player decides from the outset to go genocideless. He later IDs
some scrolls as genocide, and blanks them. Later, in a tight spot, the
player has a magic marker and blank scrolls, but does not use them for
a convenient genocide. He later ascends. "You did not genocide any
monsters."

2)  A player has no intention of adhering to any conducts. As it so
happens, he never ends up IDing the genocide scroll. He uses his
wishes on other items, without any particular intention to avoid the
genocide scroll. At times, a convenient genocide would have been
helpful, but the player doesn't have the means or knowledge to obtain
a genocide scroll. He later ascends. "You did not genocide any
monsters."

3)  A player decides from the outset to go genocideless. As it so
happens, he never ends up IDing the genocide scroll. He uses his
wishes on other items, partially because his chosen conduct makes the
genocide scroll useless to him. Later, in a tight spot, the player has
a magic marker and blank scrolls, but he couldn't use them to write a
genocide scroll even if he wanted to, because he has never IDd that
scroll. He later ascends. "You did not genocide any monsters."

4)  A player has no intention to observe any conducts. Some time into
the game, the player finds and IDs the genocide scroll. He holds onto
it for the present, intending to use it later on when needed. He
builds up AC and resistances, obtains artifact weapons and learns
spells. At this point, the player begins thinking he may be able to
ascend without using genocide. He gets into a tight spot at one point
in which genocide would be convenient, but resists the temptation and
gets past that point. He later ascends. "You did not genocide any
monsters."

The question is, which of these are "legitimate" conduct ascensions?
According to the game's reckoning, all four are. If intentionality is
the crucial factor, then only games one and three are. If resisted
opportunity is the crucial factor, then only games one and four are.
If intentionality and resisted opportunity are both necessary, then
only game one is.

I've used genocide as the hypothetical example because it involves
fewer complications than other conducts. For example, wishes are
always possible once someone clears the castle. There is also a
guaranteed amulet of lifesaving in the game, and it is a matter of
debate whether "survivor" conduct is broken by putting on an AoLS or
by not actually having one's life saved by one. So let's stick to
genocide or a similar simple example.

So, what say ye? Which of these ascensions is a "true" genocideless
conduct ascension?
